Z||000000||Bluebottlejunky||02-04-2001||06:54 PM||||How hard is it to find a Oil Pan for a 85 GT, is it the same as all other years of fieros? How much do they cost?<P>Mike<BR>Got Fiero? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||02-04-2001||07:07 PM||||if no one else answers with a for sure, id say since the engines are all identical, all the oil pans should interchange. exception may be 88 since they changed cradle and suspension a little.<P>Now Im wondering if someone like Summit makes an oversize pan, Id like to carry a little more oil capacity? Ive even considered putting dual remote oil filters and a cooler behind the quarter panel scoop on pass side.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||02-05-2001||11:26 AM||||I believe the 87/88 v-6s have a different timing cover and oil pan arrangement then the 85/86s.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Got2manyfieros||02-05-2001||01:29 PM||||The 87-88 oil pan and timing cover is indeed different from the 85-86.  The fiero store carries new oil pans, and I believe you can still get them from GM as well.<BR>I think I paid somewhere in the neighborhood of 80 dollars for mine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||FIEROGOD||02-07-2001||09:42 PM|||| I have put several motors in fieros. GM was very nice in that they used the same oilpan on all front wheel drive cars with cast-iron head 2.8s except the mini vans which were 3.1s. The fiero 2.8 block setup is the same as most if not all first gen 2.8s. I have put a motor in a 87 GT (GT BASTARD'S new gt) and the only difference that i noticed is the type of bolts used in the timeing cover (they use torx on 87 and 88 2.8s). You should be able to get an oil pan off of any 80ish through 87 front wheel drive 2.8 or any 2.8 fiero. IT WILL WORK!  ||||reg||1
